refer to the project in this repo: https://github.com/firstlast20200202/kodekloud-postman

download and install node.js
$ git clone https://github.com/firstlast20200202/kodekloud-postman.git .
$ npm i
$ node lesson1/index.js
listening on port 4000    //output
$ curl localhost:4000
{"status":"success"}      //output                   

in Postman:
+ HTTP request (instead of MCP request) 
GET localhost:4000/products
POST localhost:4000/products     //required parameters in body could be found in the code as below
//requires "name", "price" and "category"
app.post("/products", async (req, res) => {
  const { name, price, category } = req.body;

code icon </>
it shows the code that we would need to generate the request, different language can be selected in the dropdown.

using sessions and cookies for authentication:
$ node lesson2/index.js
login request generates a cookie, deleting it via the 'cookie' link under 'Send' button would logout.

using json web token for authentication:
$ node lesson3/index.js
login request generates a token, copy the token, before sending GET localhost:4000/products, 
select 'Bearer Toekn' in 'Authorization' tab, paste the token, then send. 
in this way we need to copy and paste the token to every request.
instead we set the token in 'authorization' in Collection level.
